1
00:00:00,370 --> 00:00:01,962
What if you could test a new rule

2
00:00:01,895 --> 00:00:04,338
on the past, before it goes live?

3
00:00:06,370 --> 00:00:08,312
We want to change our expense rules.

4
00:00:08,332 --> 00:00:10,200
So, a quick bet:

5
00:00:10,220 --> 00:00:12,350
out of 600 past decisions,

6
00:00:12,370 --> 00:00:14,688
how many would now get a different answer?

7
00:00:15,383 --> 00:00:18,775
The answer: 147 out of 600.

8
00:00:19,206 --> 00:00:20,698
Almost one in four.

9
00:00:21,478 --> 00:00:24,396
A small rule change, with a big footprint.

10
00:00:25,383 --> 00:00:26,750
So which sentence did it?

11
00:00:26,770 --> 00:00:28,850
One clause, the receipt rule,

12
00:00:28,870 --> 00:00:31,450
accounts for 48 of those changes.

13
00:00:33,382 --> 00:00:34,737
But here's the twist.

14
00:00:34,757 --> 00:00:39,200
38 of the 147 already broke the old rulebook.

15
00:00:39,554 --> 00:00:41,172
The new rule didn't cause them.

16
00:00:41,192 --> 00:00:43,184
Only 109 are its doing.

17
00:00:45,370 --> 00:00:48,225
And every change is one click from its evidence:

18
00:00:48,245 --> 00:00:51,450
the facts on the day, and what each rulebook said.

19
00:00:55,370 --> 00:00:57,188
Someone was promoted last year.

20
00:00:57,208 --> 00:00:58,150
Should that change what they

21
00:00:58,083 --> 00:00:59,825
could claim two years ago?

22
00:01:02,370 --> 00:01:03,400
Of course not.

23
00:01:03,420 --> 00:01:06,375
So the replay only uses what was known on the day.

24
00:01:06,395 --> 00:01:08,137
Use today's facts instead,

25
00:01:08,157 --> 00:01:10,425
and 39 answers come out wrong.

26
00:01:12,407 --> 00:01:14,612
Point-in-time replay keeps tomorrow's

27
00:01:14,545 --> 00:01:17,175
knowledge out of yesterday's decisions.

28
00:01:20,382 --> 00:01:22,550
The whole machine is four steps.

29
00:01:22,782 --> 00:01:24,487
Remember the old facts.

30
00:01:24,954 --> 00:01:26,772
Replay both rulebooks.

31
00:01:27,114 --> 00:01:29,593
Ask a person about the cases that matter.

32
00:01:29,838 --> 00:01:31,194
Then check every future

33
00:01:31,126 --> 00:01:32,993
rule against their answers.

34
00:01:35,370 --> 00:01:38,675
Airflow runs each step as a scheduled workflow.

35
00:01:41,370 --> 00:01:44,175
The evidence lands in one shared database,

36
00:01:44,195 --> 00:01:46,487
and this dashboard reads it back.

37
00:01:50,370 --> 00:01:52,987
Nobody has to read 600 cases.

38
00:01:53,007 --> 00:01:56,262
The demo picks just eight for a person to judge.

39
00:01:58,370 --> 00:02:00,150
Each answer becomes a precedent:

40
00:02:00,170 --> 00:02:03,100
a test every future rule must pass.

41
00:02:03,120 --> 00:02:04,125
Reverse one,

42
00:02:04,145 --> 00:02:07,000
and the check fails until somebody explains why.

43
00:02:09,370 --> 00:02:10,375
And it's fair.

44
00:02:10,395 --> 00:02:13,062
If the old policy already broke that precedent,

45
00:02:13,082 --> 00:02:15,338
the new rule doesn't take the blame.

46
00:02:15,358 --> 00:02:17,575
A red result needs a real reason,

47
00:02:17,595 --> 00:02:18,937
not a scapegoat.

48
00:02:20,370 --> 00:02:22,187
Still arguing about the limit?

49
00:02:22,207 --> 00:02:25,500
50 pounds? 100? 150?

50
00:02:27,370 --> 00:02:28,962
Sweep them all at once.

51
00:02:28,982 --> 00:02:30,787
Each row shows how many decisions

52
00:02:30,720 --> 00:02:32,950
would change, and what it costs.

53
00:02:36,383 --> 00:02:38,250
These use the offline rules.

54
00:02:38,270 --> 00:02:41,025
They show the trade-offs, not a recommendation.

55
00:02:41,045 --> 00:02:42,562
The choice stays with you.

56
00:02:45,370 --> 00:02:46,800
So, would you ship this rule?

57
00:02:46,820 --> 00:02:49,475
Now you know who it affects, what it costs,

58
00:02:49,495 --> 00:02:51,850
and which decisions it must respect.

59
00:02:54,383 --> 00:02:55,725
Policy Time Machine.

60
00:02:55,745 --> 00:02:58,625
Try tomorrow's rules on yesterday's decisions.
